' THE DUKE OF DRAGONFEAR
' From Tim Hartnell's Giant Book of Computer Games (Ballantine Books,
' 1984; Interface Publications, 1983), for the ColecoVision in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu. A development of Gregory Yob's Hunt the Wumpus.
'
' Explore the caves of Dragonworld, an 8 x 8 grid, looking for treasure
' and slaying the fearsome dragons who live there. You start in cave 55
' with 25 units of charisma and lose one for every move. Among the caves
' are solid walls, magic caves that whisk you somewhere else, dragons'
' lairs, quicksand, and hoards of dragon-gold. Your magic amulet tells
' you what lies in one of the eight caves around you, but not which.
' From time to time you get a glimpse of the whole cave system.
'
' Controls (either joystick): the stick moves you north, south, east or
' west; the button fights a dragon (then the stick aims the arrow); the
' second button quits. Your name is picked with the stick on the first
' screen. Keypad not used.
'
' Liberties taken:
' - The book's dragon used the charisma counter for its dice (resetting
'   your charisma) and an unset variable, so a dragon that did not fly
'   away always killed you; here it flies away one time in five, has
'   recently eaten and goes back to sleep in 15 per cent of the rest,
'   and you fight back and win one time in twenty, as the text intends.
' - The book announced "All your charisma is exhausted" when quicksand
'   or a dragon ended the game; here each ending says what happened.
' - Caves you have visited stay shown on the map; the glimpses show
'   everything for a moment, as the book's printed map did. The best
'   haul of gold is kept. New music (Dragonworld) and sound effects.
'
' House rules kept: columns 2-29, rows 1-22; four sprites at most (the
' Duke in two colours and an arrow); the frame loop is protected from the
' NMI and every screen is drawn with the NMI off after a wait.

